{
 "lead": "Bell's inequality turns a philosophical question into a number. If each particle carried a pre-set answer, a local hidden variable, certain correlation sums cannot exceed 2. Quantum mechanics predicts up to 2√2. The CHSH game makes the same gap visible as a win probability: 3/4 classically, about 0.8536 with entanglement. Labs use these ideas in Bell tests, and the game is a standard way to see why entanglement is a resource.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#quantum-measurement\">quantum measurement</a> and the Born rule, plus <a href=\"#entanglement-bell-states-and-the-epr-paradox\">Bell states and entanglement</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "The CHSH game",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A referee sends one bit x to Alice and one bit y to Bob. Each bit is 0 or 1, chosen uniformly and independently. Alice returns a bit a and Bob returns a bit b. They may agree on a strategy before the game, but after receiving x and y they cannot communicate. They win when a ⊕ b = x · y. Here ⊕ is XOR (addition modulo 2), and x · y is AND, which is 1 only when x = y = 1."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "x",
      "y",
      "x·y",
      "Win when"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "0",
       "0",
       "0",
       "a = b"
      ],
      [
       "0",
       "1",
       "0",
       "a = b"
      ],
      [
       "1",
       "0",
       "0",
       "a = b"
      ],
      [
       "1",
       "1",
       "1",
       "a ≠ b"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For three input pairs the answers must match; for (1,1) they must differ. A classical local strategy gives Alice and Bob shared random data λ, and each answer depends only on the player's own input and λ. Any fixed λ gives a deterministic strategy. No deterministic strategy can satisfy all four rows: making a = b always fails at (1,1), and making a ≠ b always fails at the other three. So at most three rows win. Shared randomness is an average of deterministic strategies, so the classical win probability is at most 3/4. The strategy 'both always answer 0' reaches 3/4."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Correlations and the CHSH value",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Use ±1 outcomes. Write A_x = (−1)^a for Alice's outcome when her input is x, and B_y = (−1)^b for Bob. Both A_x and B_y are observables with eigenvalues +1 and −1; the bit 0 maps to +1 and the bit 1 maps to −1. Their product is AB = +1 when a = b and −1 when a ≠ b. Define the correlation E(x,y) = ⟨A_x B_y⟩. For a fixed input pair, E = P(a = b) − P(a ≠ b), so P(a = b) = (1 + E)/2 and P(a ≠ b) = (1 − E)/2."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "S = E(0,0) + E(0,1) + E(1,0) − E(1,1)",
     "where": "E(x,y) is the correlation for Alice input x and Bob input y; S is the CHSH value."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Local hidden variables obey |S| ≤ 2. This is the CHSH inequality, one form of Bell's inequality. Quantum mechanics obeys a different bound, |S| ≤ 2√2. The number 2√2 ≈ 2.828 is Tsirelson's bound. A measured S above 2 cannot be explained by any local hidden-variable model of that experiment. Some syllabi call these Bell inequality violation experiments. The CHSH game converts S into a win probability. For the three inputs with x·y = 0, Pwin = (1 + E)/2. For (1,1), Pwin = (1 − E)/2. A strategy with E(0,0) = E(0,1) = E(1,0) = 1/√2 and E(1,1) = −1/√2 has S = 2√2. Then every input pair has Pwin = (1 + 1/√2)/2 = (2 + √2)/4 ≈ 0.8536."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Quantum strategy with |Φ+⟩",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Take the Bell state |Φ+⟩ = (|00⟩ + |11⟩)/√2. Alice and Bob each hold one qubit. Use the Pauli operators Z = diag(1,−1) and X = [[0,1],[1,0]]. A measurement of Z or X returns +1 or −1. For input x = 0 Alice measures Z; for x = 1 she measures X. For input y = 0 Bob measures B0 = (Z + X)/√2; for y = 1 he measures B1 = (Z − X)/√2. These Bob observables are Hermitian and square to the identity, so their outcomes are also ±1."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "(x,y)",
      "E(x,y)",
      "Pwin"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "(0,0)",
       "1/√2",
       "0.8536"
      ],
      [
       "(0,1)",
       "1/√2",
       "0.8536"
      ],
      [
       "(1,0)",
       "1/√2",
       "0.8536"
      ],
      [
       "(1,1)",
       "−1/√2",
       "0.8536"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The correlations follow from ⟨Z⊗Z⟩ = 1, ⟨X⊗X⟩ = 1, and ⟨Z⊗X⟩ = ⟨X⊗Z⟩ = 0 for |Φ+⟩. For example, E(0,0) = ⟨Z⊗(Z + X)/√2⟩ = (1 + 0)/√2 = 1/√2. For (1,1), E(1,1) = ⟨X⊗(Z − X)/√2⟩ = (0 − 1)/√2 = −1/√2. Substituting in S gives 2√2. The winning probability is the same for all four inputs: (2 + √2)/4 ≈ 0.8536. This beats 3/4. Alice's own outcome is still uniformly random, and Bob's input does not change Alice's marginal, so the strategy sends no signal."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
